3.4.6.m:
'May the years, O Agni, may the seasons make you grow,' [he says];
he kindles him with the years and with the seasons.
'Shine forth with the divine brightness,' [he says];
he piles him luminous indeed.
'Shine across all the four directions,' [he says];
therefore Agni shines across all the directions.
'When from being there you freed [him] from the curse of Yama, O Bṛhaspati,' [with this] he should act for someone who is ill.
Yama is death;
Bṛhaspati is the holy power;
with as much as the holy power is, with that he heals him.
'May the two Aśvins thrust death away from him,' [he says];
the Aśvins are the two physicians of the gods;
they thrust death away from him.
'Up from the darkness we [have risen],' [he says];
having struck away the darkness of evil, he ascends to the heavenly world.
Ahṛta Haitanāmana was the Hotṛ of Sarvatir Āpāṣṭhi for the fire-altar to be piled.
They said to him:
'Seek those [verses] that contain [the word] water.'
To him the gandharvas and apsarases declared them.
When the kindling verses contain [the word] water,
[it is] for appeasement.
He recites twenty-four;
twenty-four syllables has the gāyatrī;
the gāyatrī is the mouth of the sacrifice;
he takes hold of the mouth of the sacrifice.
Moreover, twenty-four half-months make a year;
this year is established prosperity;
he does not fall away from prosperity,
nor does his prosperity fall away.
He recites gāyatrī [verses];
the gāyatrī is brilliance and sacred luster;
he secures brilliance and sacred luster.
He recites triṣṭubh [verses];
the triṣṭubh is force and heroic strength;
he secures force and heroic strength.
Because there are both gāyatrīs and triṣṭubhs,
he thereby secures both.
Those addressed to Agni that are unexpressed should be used;
for this is unexpressed, as it were, undivided;
he departs divided from evil, from his rival.
He should perform them for the victim belonging to Agni and Soma.
For who would ascend the domain of the gods twice?
They say: 'Having borne [it] for three nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Three-footed is the virāj;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for six nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Six are the seasons of the year;
the virāj depends upon the year;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for ten nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Ten syllables has the virāj;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for twelve nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Twelve months make a year;
the virāj depends upon the year;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for twenty-four nights, the fire is to be piled.'
Twenty-four half-months make a year;
the virāj depends upon the year;
he obtains the virāj.
They say: 'Having borne [it] for a month, the fire is to be piled.'
Month by month he obtains the year.
For eight months the Vasus bore this;
they won this world, the gāyatrī meter.
For eleven [months] the Rudras [bore it];
they won the atmosphere world, the triṣṭubh meter.
For twelve [months] the Ādityas [bore it];
they won that world, the jagatī meter.
They prospered.
Prosper does he who, knowing thus, bears the fire for a year.
Therefore they say:
'The fire is borne for a year indeed.'
Now these āprī verses:
Prajāpati, having created offspring, thought himself emptied.
He saw these āprī verses;
with them he pleased himself.
Agni is Prajāpati;
when there are these āprī verses,
the sacrificer pleases Agni with them.
This meter is hairy, related to cattle, deficient and redundant,
for procreation.
They are virāj [verses];
the virāj is all the meters;
all his meters are piled.
He should make them all-piled.
These are the vital breaths, larger, as it were, and smaller, as it were;
for these vital breaths are larger, as it were, and smaller, as it were;
he places the vital breaths in himself.

3.4.7.m:
If someone's pan-fire goes out, it is just as if a son, once born, were to die.
If it should go out, he should rekindle that very pan.
For this is Agni's womb;
he generates him from his own womb.
Indeed, he who piles the fire-altar is liable to give birth.
'Whatever moist wombs are yours, O Agni, whatever are nested, whatever drops are yours, O Agni, and whatever navels,
with both of these, being in agreement, knowing, sit down here with your body,'
[with these words he touches it].
In that it is moist [and] possessing a womb,
[it is] for pacification.
A nest is offspring;
a nest is cattle.
In that they are nested,
[it is] for procreation.
Indeed, he who piles the fire-altar becomes an oblation.
If he who has piled the fire-altar approaches someone's woman, it would be just as if an oblation were spilt.
Just as they seek an expiation for a spilt oblation, so they should seek an expiation for him.
If he should approach [her], he should sacrifice with a curd-mixture for Mitra and Varuṇa;
he enters into the state of being belonging to Mitra and Varuṇa,
so that he himself is not spilt.
Agni is an animal.
Whoever approaches an animal from the front harms it;
therefore he does not step from the front facing west;
and therefore he approaches from behind facing east,
for non-injury to himself.
He who piles the fire-altar according to the turns, according to form offspring and seasons are arranged for him.
Those that turn to the right he places on the south; those that turn to the left, on the north;
those marked with three lines he places in the back facing east.
He thus piles the fire-altar according to the turns; according to form offspring and seasons are arranged for him.
They say: 'The fire-altar must be piled with truth.'
'Bhūḥ, bhuvaḥ, svaḥ,' [saying this], the naturally perforated [brick is placed] in front;
for this is as much as truth is in speech;
he piles the fire-altar with truth itself.
The naturally perforated [brick] is the breath;
gold is immortality.
When, having placed a gold brick, he places the naturally perforated [brick],
he places his breaths in immortality itself.
'You are brilliance; grant me brilliance,' [with this] he places the gold bricks.
By these indeed these worlds are held apart;
and by these he piles the fire-altar;
these worlds shine forth.
The gods, having slain the Asuras, were afraid of death.
They saw the meters;
they entered them.
Whatever covered them, with that they covered themselves;
that is the meter-ness of the meters.
Having placed layer after layer, he touches the domain, the meter, with [a verse] addressed to Agni;
he enters into his own deity,
for non-injury to himself.
One desiring heaven should pile the falcon-pile;
for having become a falcon, the gāyatrī flew to yonder world;
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
One who has a rival should pile the chariot-wheel-pile;
for having made a chariot-wheel a thunderbolt, the gods rolled it toward the Asuras;
they crushed them with a hundredfold blow.
Making this a thunderbolt, the sacrificer rolls it toward his rival;
he crushes [him] with a hundredfold blow.
One who has a rival should pile the fore-part-of-a-shaft-pile;
for the Asuras had surrounded the gods from both sides, some in front, some behind;
they drove them away with this;
this is indeed the driving away of the rival.
One desiring food should pile the trough-pile;
for food is eaten from a trough;
by what corresponds to it he secures food.
There is a pot behind,
for correspondence.
One who desires, 'May I go straight to the world of the fathers,' should pile the burial-mound-pile;
he goes straight to the world of the fathers.
And one desiring a village should pile beside another;
just as he piles this up, so he piles up a village for him.
One desiring cattle should pile the swept-together-pile;
just as he sweeps this together, so he sweeps together cattle for him from the directions.

3.4.10.m:
He who piles the fire-altar without a womb, without an abode, becomes without a womb, without an abode.
Water is the womb of the fire.
In that he lays down the pot-bricks,
he piles the fire with a womb, with an abode; he becomes possessed of a womb, possessed of an abode.
'The fire-altar must be piled with its rival beneath,' they say.
Water is the rival of the fire.
In that he lays down the pot-bricks,
he piles the fire with its rival beneath; evil becomes beneath him, his rival beneath him.
Fire is grief;
water is pacification.
In that he lays down the pot-bricks,
[it is] for pacification.
In that there are male pots and female pots, that is a pair;
in that they are in couples,
[it is] for propagation.
He lays down twelve;
twelve months make the year;
reaching the year, he secures it.
A mess of wild rice is the thirteenth;
there is a thirteenth month;
reaching that with this, he secures it.
It is in milk;
by what is milk,
he secures cultivated food;
by what is wild rice,
[he secures] wild food;
thereby he secures both of them.
Thus ends the fourth prapāṭhaka on the shower of wealth in the upper book.

3.5.1.m:
He indeed cuts himself off from Prajāpati, who lays down the head and not the other limbs.
His [altar] becomes a bone-pile, a pile-layer on a burial mound.
In that he lays down this man-pile,
he does not cut himself off from Prajāpati;
his [altar] becomes not a bone-pile, not a pile-layer on a burial mound.
He lays down thirty-six of these;
thirty-six syllables has the bṛhatī;
cattle belong to the bṛhatī;
man belongs to the bṛhatī.
The likeness of the man is laid down,
for he is thus placed.
And this sacrificer indeed does not die prematurely into that world, for whom these are laid down.

3.5.2.m:
Now these pañcāpañcīna [bricks]:
The fire to be piled is loose, as it were.
In that he lays down the pañcāpañcīna [bricks],
[it is] for the firmness of the fire, for non-looseness.
He lays down five;
fivefold is the sacrifice.
As great as the sacrifice is, so great he takes hold of.
'You are the abundance-maker,
you are the wide-space-maker,' [he says];
he places abundance right in the fire;
he becomes greater in offspring, in cattle.
'You sit in the waters,
you sit like a vulture,' [he says];
the fire is indeed a bird;
he makes it a bird capable of sight.
Having become a bird, he goes to the heavenly world, for whom these are laid down.

3.6.1.m:
He should offer an eleven-potsherd [cake] for Agni and Viṣṇu.
Agni is all the deities;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
He takes hold of both the deities and the sacrifice.
Agni is the end of the sacrifice from below;
Viṣṇu is from the front.
From both sides he takes hold of the ends of the sacrifice.
Regarding this they say:
'Let there be no sacrifice without Indra.'
In that it is on eight potsherds, by that it belongs to Agni;
in that it is on three potsherds, by that it belongs to Viṣṇu;
in that it amounts to eleven potsherds, by that it belongs to Indra.
Agni is the purifier of the sacrifice;
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice.
He takes hold of the sacrifice purified by the purifier.
This is the form of the sacrificial substance of the sacrifice: namely, the cake;
therefore, a cake alone is to be made.
[Some] make two carus.
Clarified butter and milk belong to the cow;
rice-grains belong to the ox.
That is a pair;
he places a pair right at the mouth of his sacrifice.
Clarified butter is brilliance;
he is born from brilliance.
This man is taken hold of for the sacrificial substance;
for this is the likeness, as it were, of a man,
since the form is from it, as it were;
therefore, a caru alone is to be made.
He consecrates himself on [ground] possessing an altar.
With 'May I find!' he consecrates himself on [ground] possessing an altar.
He indeed finds who consecrates himself on [ground] possessing an altar.
Moreover, he has consecrated himself right on the path of the sacrifice.
By the altar the gods found this [earth] from the Asuras;
he indeed finds this [earth] who consecrates himself on [ground] possessing an altar.
Moreover, he takes hold of the path of the sacrifice.
They make a hall with the roof-beam pointing eastward.
When they partitioned these quarters, they partitioned this [eastern quarter] for the gods, this for the Fathers, this for the Asuras, this for men.
He who is consecrated approaches the deity;
he turns toward the quarter of the gods;
he turns toward the eastern quarter.
Moreover, he moves into the domain of the gods.
They enclose it.
Hidden from the divine abode is the human abode;
they hide him from the human abode.
Moreover, [it is] for non-approach by the Rakṣases.
He who is consecrated departs from this world,
for he goes to the people [of the gods];
he ascends toward the world of the gods.
While enclosing, they make openings;
thereby he does not depart from this world;
thereby he is held fast in this world.
He should make the entrance in the front for one desiring heaven;
that sun is the heavenly world;
he brings him into the presence of that sun.
He should make the entrance in the south for one whom he might desire: 'May he prosper in the world of the Fathers';
that is the quarter of the Fathers;
he prospers right in the world of the Fathers.
He should make the entrance in the west for one desiring offspring;
in the west seed is placed;
the consecrated one is seed;
he places seed in him.
He should make the entrance in the north for one whom he might desire: 'May he prosper in the world of men';
that is the quarter of men;
he prospers right in the world of men.
He should make the entrance in the north-east for one whom he might desire: 'May he prosper in both worlds';
that belongs to both worlds;
he prospers right in both worlds.
He should make the entrance on all sides for one whom he might desire: 'May he prosper in all quarters';
he prospers in all quarters.

3.6.3.m:
He salves [his eyes] toward the front,
for procreation.
He salves with a reed-stalk;
for human beings salve with a quill.
He salves with one possessing a tuft;
for human beings salve with one devoid of a tuft.
He salves the right [eye] first;
for human beings salve the left first.
He salves three times on one, three times on the other;
for human beings salve without measure.
He does not apply it repeatedly;
for human beings salve with recurring turns.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
his pupil flew away;
it went to the three-peaked [mountain].
He salves with that, salve from the three-peaked [mountain].
Truth is the eye;
he does not place faith in speech, as it were;
having grasped truth alone, he enters upon consecration.
'Between us, with you, I place hatred, between us [I place] enemies with the great mountain,' [he says];
with a mountain he puts hatred and enemies between.
'You are the protector of sight;
protect my sight,' he says;
he invokes a blessing.
He purifies him upward and downward;
for this breath is both upward and downward.
If he should hate someone, he should purify him obliquely;
he confuses his breaths;
he becomes liable to die.
'May the lord of thought purify you,' he says;
the lord of thought is the sacrifice.
'May the lord of speech purify you,' he says;
the lord of speech purifies him for the sacrifice.
'May the divine Savitṛ purify you,' he says;
impelled by Savitṛ, he purifies himself for the sacrificial substance through these deities.
'With an unbroken strainer,' he says;
this unbroken strainer is the rays of the sun;
with an unbroken strainer he purifies him.
'With your strainer, O lord of the strainer,' [he says];
the lord of the strainer is the sacrifice.
Truly, for the sacrifice he purifies himself.
'May I master the sacrifice,' he says.
Indra slew Vṛtra on the waters;
what was worthy of sacrifice and fit for sacrifice among them, that stepped upward;
those became these plants.
Their brilliance is darbha-grass;
these are dried waters;
whatever brilliance they have, that he secures.
Threefold are the waters: celestial, earthly, belonging to the ocean;
darbha-grass has pervaded them all;
therefore darbha-grass is a strainer.
He purifies with two;
two are the strainers;
two-footed is the sacrificer;
as great as his self is, that much he purifies.
He purifies three times;
for the gods have threefold truth;
and these breaths are three: prāṇa, apāna, vyāna;
as great as his self is, that much he purifies.
He purifies with seven;
seven are the meters;
he purifies him with the meters;
moreover, the meters are sacred formulation;
he purifies him with sacred formulation.
He purifies with twenty-one;
ten are the fingers of the hands, ten of the feet;
the self is the twenty-first;
as great as his self is, that much he purifies.
Three are connected with destruction: dice, women, sleep.
In that he consecrates himself, thereby he turns away from dice and women.
In that the consecrated one stays awake the first night, thereby he turns away from sleep.
In that the consecrated one spends the first night, thereby he secures the sacrifice;
when the Soma is bought, [the night] he [spends], offspring by that;
[the night] when the morrow's pressing [takes place], cattle by that.

3.7.2.m:
The other butter-portions are sacrificed with 'enjoying', but here he sacrifices on both sides with a verse,
for firmness, for lack of slackness.
Seventeen enkindling verses are to be used:
five seasons, twelve months—that is the year;
from the year indeed he extends the mouth of the sacrifice.
Regarding this they say:
"Fifteen enkindling verses are to be used,
not seventeen;
only for the yearly course are seventeen to be used,
for the prosperity of the year."
That is not to be heeded:
seventeen indeed are to be used.
The introductory [offering] should have fore-offerings [and] be without after-offerings;
the concluding [offering] should have after-offerings [and] be without fore-offerings.
On both sides indeed the sacrifice is joined by the fore-offerings and after-offerings,
for this sacrifice is the same.
He deposits the scraping and the stirring-stick;
by that the sacrifice is made continuous.
The self indeed is the fore-offerings;
the domain of the gods is the fore-offerings and after-offerings.
The sacrifice, stepping into the domain of the gods, would exclude offspring if he should omit the after-offerings.
The introductory [offering] should have fore-offerings [and] have after-offerings;
the concluding [offering] should have after-offerings [and] have fore-offerings.
In milk should be the introductory [offering]; in milk the concluding [offering].
This is the bright essence of the waters;
he obtains the bright essence of the waters.
Cattle indeed are the bright essence;
he obtains cattle;
and also he places cattle for him at the entrance and at the conclusion.
Sacred to Aditi should be the introductory [offering]; sacred to Aditi the concluding [offering].
This [earth] indeed is Aditi;
on her indeed he stands firmly with the mouth of the sacrifice;
on her above.
Where indeed at the beginning half of the sacrifice what is prosperous is done, whatever is being done prospers for him.
The introductory [offering] is to be completed,
for the prosperity of the sacrifice.
The verses that are invitatory verses at the introductory [offering] should be the offering verses at the concluding [offering];
those that are invitatory verses at the concluding [offering] should be the offering verses at the introductory [offering],
for recognizing the path,
and also for continuous connection.
If they should do otherwise than this, they would make an interruption.

3.7.4.m:
That which is of two colors is Vṛtra-slaying.
If he should buy with that, his realm would become victorious.
Now, one whose funeral cow is such, another of them quickly dies.
That which is red, black-eyed, black-tailed, black-hoofed belongs to the fathers.
If he should buy with that, the sacrificer would be prone to die.
Now, one whose funeral cow is such, another of them dies in due season.
That which is reddish-brown, brown-haired, white-glancing, bright-eyed—that is the form of the cow that buys Soma.
With her own proper form indeed is [Soma] bought.
She should not be one-eyed, not stunted, not lame, not seven-hoofed;
thus with a complete [cow] is it bought.
With a one-year-old [cow] it is to be bought.
Speech indeed is the cow that buys Soma;
therefore creatures speak speech at [the age of] a year.
It is to be bought in waters.
Plants indeed are Soma;
water is the sap of plants;
thus that sap is produced.
"In buying Soma indeed do I secure the third pressing;
I know [this],"
said Aruṇa Aupaveśi.
Animals indeed are the third pressing;
the skin is of animals;
in that he pours it out on the skin,
thereby he secures the third pressing.
He pours it out on a red [skin];
therefore the form of animals is red.
He pours it out on [the skin of] a draft-ox;
the draft-ox indeed is all the ages of life of animals;
having attained all the ages of life of animals, he secures [them];
moreover, he secures much of the sacrifice.
They pick through the Soma,
for the discrimination of the worse and the better;
moreover, they purify it for the gods.
The Adhvaryu should not pick through the Soma,
nor the sacrificer,
nor the men of the sacrificer, nor should there be onlookers of it being picked through.
If there should be onlookers of it being picked through, creatures would incur hunger;
distress would seize the sacrificer; the Adhvaryu would become hungry.
"Purify the Soma! Throw out what has fallen into distress!" he should say.
Swallowed indeed is that of Soma which has fallen into distress;
those who pick through the Soma tear out the swallowed [portion] of Soma.
Therefore sellers of Soma, though buying much and finding much, are hungry,
for they tear out the swallowed [portion] of Soma.
With "You are ours," [he touches it];
this is his stalking [of it].
With "Bright are your cups,"
he grasps the bright [portion] of it.
With "Towards that god Savitṛ,"
impelled by Savitṛ he grasps [it].
He grasps with an atichandas-verse;
all the meters indeed are the atichandas;
with all the meters indeed he grasps it.
The highest point of the meters indeed is this [verse];
he makes him go to the highest point of his equals.
With five [fingers] he grasps;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
as great as the sacrifice is, so great he has undertaken.
He measures [it], letting go each single [finger];
with [powers] that are not exhausted he measures.
For only thus far is the virility of each of them attained.
Therefore he extends these at will;
he bends [them] back at will.
Ten times he measures;
of ten syllables is the virāj;
he attains the virāj;
moreover, animals belong to the virāj;
he secures animals.
Twice he grasps [portions];
for there are two pressings.
As much Soma as is grasped, that belongs to the sacrificer;
what he heaps up, that belongs to the members of the assembly.
With "For offspring, you!" he heaps [it] up;
for they creep forward from family to family.
Plants indeed are Soma;
unmeasured is livelihood;
if he were to grasp a measured [amount], livelihood would be measured;
now, in that he heaps [it] up,
[it is] for the securing of the unmeasured.
The sacrificer indeed is Prajāpati;
the shoots are creatures;
in that he ties up the Soma,
he ties up the breath of creatures.
In that he says,
"May offspring breathe after you,"
he establishes breath in them.
He ties it up in linen;
linen indeed is made of plants;
plants are the womb of Soma;
he places him in his own womb.
When the sacrifice first went to the gods, the rakṣases wished to slay it;
it crept into the deities.
Of all the deities indeed is the garment;
in that he ties [it] up in linen,
with all the deities he smites away the rakṣases from the sacrifice;
moreover, with all the deities he makes the sacrifice prosper.

3.7.5.m:
There is clarified butter taken four times.
Four-footed indeed are cattle;
he secures cattle.
Having placed gold [into it], he sacrifices.
Clarified butter belongs to Agni;
gold is born of Agni.
With its very body, with its very brilliance, it is offered.
This indeed is Agni's dear body, namely clarified butter;
brilliance is gold.
When he sacrifices having placed gold [into it],
he makes Agni's dear body swell with brilliance;
and also he causes him to grasp the forms.
Clarified butter indeed is cattle;
semen is gold.
When he sacrifices having placed gold [into it],
he places semen into cattle.
Therefore from boneless semen boned cattle are born.
He sacrifices with [the gold] bound [to a string];
therefore the embryos of cattle are not prone to drop.
Prone to drop would be the embryos of cattle if he should sacrifice with it unbound.
"You are swift," [he says];
when indeed she hastens, "May it be so, may it be so," that is her swiftness.
"Held by mind," [he says];
for by mind one holds speech.
"Pleasing to Viṣṇu," [he says];
he has made her pleasing to the sacrifice, to Viṣṇu.
"At the impulse of you of true impulse," [he says];
for impelled by speech [a deed] is done.
"May I obtain the restraint of the body, svāhā!" [he says];
he prays for a blessing.
"You are bright, you are shining," [saying this] he scoops it out,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Speech indeed is the cow that buys Soma;
he indeed directs her in this way.
Therefore, having produced [offspring], creatures bring forth offspring.
The newborn calf seeks the teat;
the young child seeks the teat.
"You are thought," [he says];
when indeed she reflects, "May it be so, may it be so," that is her thought.
"You are mind," [he says];
for what one thinks with the mind, what one approaches with the mind, that one speaks with speech.
"You are reflection," [he says];
when indeed she meditates, "May it be so, may it be so," that is her reflection.
"You are the sacrificial fee," [he says];
whatever indeed is given with speech, that is her nature as a sacrificial fee.
"You are worthy of sacrifice," [he says];
for speech is worthy of sacrifice.
"You are of the ruling class," [he says];
for whom speech favors, he belongs to the ruling class.
"You are Aditi, two-headed," [he says];
inasmuch as the introductory [offering] belongs to Aditi and the concluding [offering] belongs to Aditi, that is her two-headedness.
"Be well-facing-forward, well-facing-backward to us," [he says];
for she faces forward at the introductory [offering], and she faces backward at the concluding [offering].

3.7.6.m:
If he should buy with one seized by the ear, she would be Vṛtra-slaying;
if with one tied by the horn, she would belong to the gods of men;
if with one bound by the foot, she would belong to the fathers;
if with one unbound, she would be unextended.
When he says:
"May Mitra bind you by the foot,"
he restrains her with Mitra's noose.
"May Pūṣan protect the paths," [he says];
this [earth] indeed is Pūṣan;
this [earth] indeed brings together her path.
"For Indra as supervisor," [he says];
Indra indeed is the mightiest of the gods;
him he has made her supervisor.
"May your mother consent to you, may your father [consent]," [he says];
consented to by her mother, father, brother, [and] friend he buys her.
"O goddess, go to the god," [he says];
for she is a goddess, [and] Soma is a god.
"To Soma for Indra," [he says];
for she goes to Soma for Indra.
"May Rudra turn you around," [he says];
he places Rudra beyond her.
"Safely, a friend of Soma, come again," [he says];
he has seized [her] visibly.
To Rudra indeed he shuts up the cattle
when he says:
"May Rudra turn you around."
When he says:
"Safely, a friend of Soma, come again,"
he places safety upon her as she returns.
Speech indeed is the cow that buys Soma;
she enters the forms of the gods.
When he says:
"You are Vasu, you are Rudrā,"
"You are this, you are this," speech indeed says to her, whatever it is;
whatever he says here, having obtained this, he secures it.
He steps forth after six footprints;
six indeed are the days [across which] speech is distributed;
speech does not exceed the sixth day;
all speech is obtained on the sixth day;
as much as speech is, that [much] he obtains.
With the right side he steps forth along the right side;
therefore food is eaten with the right hand;
therefore the right side of the body is more vigorous;
therefore birds turn around along the right side.
In the seventh footprint he sacrifices;
seven are the meters;
speech is the meters;
as much as speech is, having obtained it by the oblation, he secures it.
Seven are the meters,
seven the Hotṛ's offices,
seven the village cattle;
those very cattle he secures.
"May Bṛhaspati delight you in favor," [he says];
Bṛhaspati indeed is the sacred power;
with the sacred power he restrains her.
"Rudra has turned [towards you] with the Vasus," [he says];
he has made Rudra with the Vasus turn towards her.
"On the head of the earth I sprinkle you," [he says];
the place of sacrifice indeed is the head of the earth.
"In the footprint of Iḍā," [he says];
the cow indeed is Iḍā;
this indeed is her footprint.
"Possessing clarified butter, svāhā!" [he says];
with the utterance of svāhā he restrains her.

3.7.7.m:
Having placed gold down, he offers.
He offers in that which has fire, in that which has a proper place.
The Adhvaryu would become blind if he were to offer in what has no proper place.
Clarified butter indeed is cattle;
cattle is the footprint.
The wooden sword is the vajra.
When he cuts around the footprint with the wooden sword,
he encloses cattle for the sacrificer with the vajra.
He cuts around after with the horn;
he encloses cattle for him having the same origin.
He pours the footprint into a pot.
From this [earth] indeed she is produced;
for this [earth] is the womb of cattle;
he places them in their own womb.
Cattle mourn when severed from the womb;
when he pours water upon [it],
he appeases it.
He hands over the footprint to the sacrificer.
Thereby the Adhvaryu becomes without cattle;
for he has handed over cattle to the sacrificer.
When he says:
"With your wealth," [he says],
thereby he makes the Adhvaryu share in cattle;
thereby the Adhvaryu, possessing cattle, deposits the footprint in his houses;
he places cattle in his houses.
The heavenly world indeed is the Āhavanīya;
if he were to scatter it near the Āhavanīya, he would place his cattle in another world.
He scatters it near the Gārhapatya;
for cattle attend near to the Gārhapatya;
he places him united with cattle.
Cattle indeed are the footprint;
Agni is Rudra;
if he were to scatter it on that which has fire, he would surrender his cattle to Rudra.
Where it is cooled down, there it should be scattered near,
for the non-burning of cattle.
When they cause the wife to look at the cow that buys Soma,
[it is] for pairing.
"Possessing Tvaṣṭṛ, with you," [he says];
for Tvaṣṭṛ fashions forms.
"May we copulate," [he says];
for from copulation offspring are born.
"I have ascended the eye of Sūrya," he says;
this indeed is the unobstructed, safe path by which that Āditya goes.
"From that path of Āditya," [he says],
he turns aside; "he finds good things," he says;
for he finds good things who buys Soma.
"O Soma-seller, let me buy your Soma, great, of much worth, shining brightly!
Let me buy yours with a sixteenth part!
Let me buy yours with a twelfth part!
Let me buy yours with a hoof!
Let me buy yours with a foot!"
Thus he proclaims the greatness of the cow;
and also he magnifies her;
and also he measures out the sacrifice syllable by syllable.
"Sūvāṅ, Nabhrāṭ, at the ember, Bambhāra," he says;
these indeed are the Soma-guardians of the gods;
from them the metres brought away Soma;
to them he points her out;
and also whatever of the sacrifice is joined here, that he buys around.
He buys with ten;
the Virāj has ten syllables;
thus it is made connected with the Virāj.
He buys with eleven;
ten indeed are the vital breaths of a sacrificial animal;
the self is the eleventh;
thus it is made whole.
He buys with a milk cow;
he buys his milk-mixture;
and also he grasps speech.
He buys with gold;
he buys his bright portion;
and also he grasps brilliance.
He buys with a garment;
he buys what belongs to all the gods;
he buys with a she-goat;
he buys his heat.
When those metres brought away Soma, they did not perceive it because of darkness;
then the Gāyatrī flew up, taking a she-goat;
she made it shine forth for them;
then indeed the metres brought away Soma.
"Bring (ājām) Soma to us": hence the she-goat (ajā).
He buys with an ox;
he buys his draft-animal;
he buys with a bull;
he buys what possesses Indra.
If he were to buy with a bull, he would sell Prajāpati away;
[Soma] is to be bought with a non-castrated young bull; he buys Indra's portion;
he does not sell Prajāpati away.
He buys with a pair of cattle;
he buys his pair.

3.7.8.m:
Soma was yonder.
Those gods sent Gāyatrī:
"Bring that Soma!"
She looked down upon the extended sacrifice;
she considered:
"If I go outside of the sacrifice, I shall go outside of myself."
Soma spoke to her this life-grasping [mantra]:
"Self-born you are,
self-existing you are."
This indeed is the life-grasping [mantra] of Soma.
He grasps this life-grasping [mantra] of Soma;
he, being the Adhvaryu, does not meet with misfortune, who knows thus.
He indeed is Varuṇa at that time, belonging to Varuṇa, when he is tied up; having become Varuṇa he would enter him.
When he says:
"Come as Mitra to us, providing good friends,"
he has made him Mitra;
he just appeases [him].
"Enter Indra's right thigh," [he says];
belonging to Indra indeed is the sacrifice;
Indra is the womb of Soma;
he places him in his own womb.
Indra indeed held him close with his thighs when he arrived at first;
following that pattern, he seats him on the thigh; he uncovers [him];
just as one uncovers when a superior comes, so is that.
"Go through the wide intermediate space," [he says];
he indeed has the intermediate space as his deity at that time,
having moved from here, not having reached yonder.
"Sit on the seat of Aditi," [he says];
belonging to Aditi indeed is the sacrifice;
Aditi is the womb of Soma;
he places him in his own womb.
Being Varuṇa, he has now made him Mitra;
thereby he deprives him of his own deity.
When he says:
"The bull propped up the sky, measured the breadth of the earth; / the sovereign sat upon all worlds; all these are the vows of Varuṇa,"
he has made him Varuṇa;
he makes him swell with his own deity.
"He spread the intermediate space among the woods," [he says]; he wraps it around with a garment;
the garment indeed belongs to all the gods;
with all the gods he encloses the bought sacrifice.
"A yoke-pin you are; injure the injurer," he says,
for the injury of the Rakṣases, for the exclusion of the Rakṣases.
"Belonging to Varuṇa you are;
may Varuṇa prop you up," [he says];
for belonging to Varuṇa is he at that time, having Varuṇa as deity.
With the mind they go towards [it];
towards his greatness indeed they go.
With a cart they carry [it];
therefore the plants ripen into fruit that is to be carried by cart.
The plants would ripen into fruit that is to be carried on the head if they were to carry the bought Soma on the head;
therefore on the mountain the plants ripen into fruit to be carried on the head;
for on the head they carry the bought Soma.
"Move forward, lord of the world," [he says];
for he is the lord of beings.
"Towards all abodes," [he says];
for towards all abodes he moves forward.
"May not highwaymen, may not waylayers, may not evil-wishing wolves find you," [he says];
where indeed the semi-Gandharva Viśvāvasu robbed him when he was being brought yonder, from such Rakṣases indeed he makes welfare for him out of fear.
He indeed is Varuṇa when bought; he is driven to the sacrificer's house;
he is liable to harm the sacrificer if not appeased.
When he says:
"Homage with the eye of Mitra and Varuṇa,"
he just appeases [him];
appeased he is driven,
for the non-harming of the sacrificer.
For all the deities indeed he is driven;
if he were to step forward with a single firebrand, his sacrifice would belong to the fathers.
A great fire is to be piled up;
Agni indeed is all the deities;
with all deities indeed he steps forward to the approaching sacrifice.
Agni and Soma indeed come together;
they would come upon the sacrificer, or upon the sacrificer's cattle;
that animal which is kept, they come upon that as their portion.
The deities indeed did not move towards that animal which was being kept;
from the one hoping in this sacrifice, which other one it was, towards that Agni and Soma moved.
Therefore this is the victim for Agni and Soma.
For Agni and Soma indeed he offers himself as a sacrificial victim, whoever is consecrated;
he redeems his cattle and himself with that very [animal].
It should be stout and fat,
for the redemption of himself.
Therefore one should not eat of the animal for Agni and Soma;
for by it the man redeems himself.
Therefore when the victim for Agni and Soma is completed, one should eat in the sacrificer's house;
then he is redeemed, free from debt.
This indeed is Vṛtra-slaying;
Soma indeed is Vṛtra;
on the previous day indeed Indra slew Vṛtra;
having attacked him on the previous day, he then presses [him] on the next day.
With the two nooses of Varuṇa indeed he is bound, whoever is consecrated;
day and night are the two nooses of Varuṇa;
if he were to complete [it] by day, he would be unreleased from the two nooses of Varuṇa;
it is to be completed at night;
he is released from the two nooses of Varuṇa,
for the non-harming of himself.

3.7.9.m:
One ox is yoked,
the other unyoked.
Then he pours out [the oblations for] the hospitality [offering],
for the continuity of the sacrifice.
He pours out from the hands of the wife.
The wife indeed rules over the household goods;
it is performed given and consented to by the wife alone.
What the wife does in the sacrifice, that is a pair;
for pairhood indeed he pours out from the hands of the wife.
This indeed is the wife's taking hold of the sacrifice, that she acts in the sacrifice;
for taking hold [of the sacrifice] indeed he pours out from the hands of the wife.
"You are the body of Agni, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
by that [formula] he takes with the gāyatrī metre.
"You are the body of Soma, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
by that with the triṣṭubh.
"You are the hospitality of the guest, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
by that with the jagatī.
"For Agni who gives increase of wealth, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
by that with the anuṣṭubh.
"For the falcon, the bringer of Soma, for Viṣṇu you!" [he says];
the falcon, the bringer of Soma, is indeed Gāyatrī;
that one indeed he takes again at this time,
not exhausted, for fivefold character.
The sacrifice is fivefold;
as great as the sacrifice is, him he has grasped.
Thus indeed he grasped the metres;
with those metres grasped, he grasps the sacrifice.
As many as follow along after a guest, of them also there is a share;
the metres indeed follow along after him from yonder;
for them this share is prepared.
The sacrifice, when bought, is Varuṇa;
Viṣṇu is [the sacrifice] extended;
extended indeed is the sacrifice at this time;
therefore it is dedicated to Viṣṇu, on nine potsherds.
Indeed, they expand it threefold at the mouth of the sacrifice; with a threefold [rite] they go forth.
Those are three on three potsherds;
[the cake] of three potsherds belongs to Viṣṇu by deity.
The gods indeed led Viṣṇu having made him a dwarf:
"As far as this one strides three times, that is ours!"
He indeed strode across this [earth] first,
then this [intermediate region], then yonder [sky];
therefore it is on three potsherds, dedicated to Viṣṇu.
This indeed is the hospitality of Soma, which is the hospitality [offering];
or rather this is the hospitality of Agni, that they churn Agni in Agni;
or rather, for the sacrifice when bought, they generate a deity;
or rather, they generate spiritual brilliance for him;
or rather, in the Upasads they generate a heroic son for him;
a heroic son is indeed born to him.
Those gods who, being Sādhyas, looked down upon the sacrifice—theirs indeed is whatever redundant [act] is performed in the sacrifice;
a redundant [act] indeed is performed in the sacrifice when they offer fire into fire;
that which is above the top-ring of the sacrificial post, that alone touches them.
They deliberate: "Is it to be brought to conclusion or not to be brought to conclusion?"
If he should bring it to conclusion, he would bring the sacrifice to conclusion at the mouth of the sacrifice.
The fore-offerings of the Upasads are the hospitality [offering]; the blessing is the Tānūnaptra;
therefore it should be made ending with the Iḍā.
The fore-offerings of the Upasads are the hospitality [offering];
therefore they are without fore-offerings.
The after-offerings of the hospitality [offering] are the Upasads;
therefore they are three;
for the after-offerings are three.
The hospitality [offering] is the head;
the Upasads are the neck;
then this other sacrifice is connected [therewith].
Prajāpati's eyelashes are these: that there are [blades of] horse-tail grass [for the prastara]; his eyebrows are the two sugarcane stems.
Prajāpati is the mouth of the sacrifice;
from the mouth of the sacrifice indeed he extends the mouth of the sacrifice.
The horse is sacrificial substance;
Prajāpati is the sacrifice;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
therefore the prastara is made of horse-tail grass.
The enclosing-sticks are made of kārṣmarya wood,
for the warding off of the Rakṣases.
